    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d799">

                <pb facs="1028" n="914" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg914"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.00 Revolutions/33: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Culbertson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-06T22:00:00-04:00">September 6, 1931—10
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>42. Embassy’s 109, September 6, 12 noon. The sale or transfer of submarines owned
                    by this Government is forbidden by Article 18 of the treaty on the limitation of
                    Naval Armament signed at Washington February 6, 1922.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d799-f1"/> As respects the delivery of war material, this Government
                    is disposed to find means of cooperation, but the Department is handicapped in
                    regard to adequate information as to the supplies available at Panama by the
                    week-end holiday, including Labor Day, and the necessity for thorough
                    consideration. It suggests that no public intimation be given of an approach to
                    us until we are able to reply fully, probably tomorrow. As respects tear gas,
                    our information is that there are no supplies of this commodity in Governmental
                    possession at Panama or elsewhere short of commercial producers in this country.
                    The delivery of munitions of war will in any case be slow and difficult, as
                    shipment of them by commercial aircraft appears to be forbidden by Article 15 of
                    the Convention on Commercial Aviation signed at Habana February 20, 1928,<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d799-f2"/> and shipment by sea is slow and obviously
                    complicated by the Chilean Naval situation.</p>
                <pb facs="1029" n="915" xml:id="pg915"/>
                <p>You may express the appreciation of this Government of the desires of the Chilean
                    Government to maintain order and stable institutions and to protect American
                    interests in Chile, and also our Government’s keen appreciation of the
                    difficulties with which Chile is confronted.</p>
                <p>Please telegraph immediately specific war material desired by the Chilean
                    Government. Point out the possibility of purchasing more adequate supplies from
                    commercial manufacturers here.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rogers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
